  "subtitle": "A synthesizer you argue with, not play",
  "blurb": "Salvatore Martirano's SAL-MAR Construction replaced a keyboard with a field of several hundred lightable capacitance touch plates. Each plate could be driven by a human finger or by the machine's own sequencer logic, because both shared the same switch bus. The performer never simply \"played\" notes; they negotiated with a semi-autonomous sequence generator, using a macro-to-micro \"zoom lens\" that could address an entire sequence tree or a single parameter from the same surface. The computer could reach back into the controls the human was using, and the human could reach into the computer's reasoning.",
